--- context ---
tools/percall_overhead_bench.py, prompted by RESEARCH.md finding 5's open
question: does the ~39-40% per-column achieved-TOPS ceiling come from fixed
per-call dispatch overhead that doesn't amortize, or a compiler/scheduling
limit inside the compute itself? Neither `vitisai_ep_report.json` (static
compile-time NPU/CPU routing only, no timing) nor the combined multi-process
throughput numbers already in this repo could distinguish the two -- this
run uses ORT's own profiler (session_options.enable_profiling) on a single
held-open 1x4.xclbin (one column) session per model, which reports a
duration for the fused `vitis_ai_ep_*_kernel_time` node (the entire on-NPU
subgraph) separately from the full per-call `model_run` duration.

Result: dispatch/sync overhead outside the compute node is negligible at
every model size tested (0.7% down to 0.1% of wall time, shrinking as
absolute call time grows -- consistent with a small roughly-fixed number of
microseconds, not a proportional cost). Essentially all wall time (95.8% to
99.5%) is the vitis_ep node's own reported duration. Node-level efficiency
against an ideal 4-TOPS column (16 TOPS nameplate / 4 columns) rises
monotonically with model size -- 19.0% (n) -> 29.3% (s) -> 36.5% (m) ->
41.3% (l) -- closely matching the combined-4-column figures already measured
by multi_partition_bench.py + estimate_tops.py (14.4% / 24.8-28.0% / 33.1% /
39.3%), a second, independent measurement method (solo per-call node timing
vs. combined multi-process throughput) landing on the same numbers.

Conclusion: this is not a dispatch-overhead-doesn't-amortize story -- that
hypothesis is ruled out directly, not just left undistinguished. The ~39-40%
ceiling and its rise with model width both live entirely inside the
compiled kernel's own scheduled execution on a single column -- a real
compiler/scheduling limit, not a fixable host-side cost. Going further would
need instruction/tile-level profiling this repo's toolchain does not expose
(see RESEARCH.md finding 5's earlier "this thread stops here" on
vitisai_ep_report.json's nodeStat lacking cycle-level detail -- same wall,
approached from a different angle, same result).
